          "thm-auslander-buchsbaum-serre-regularity-criterion": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-noetherian-local-flatness-criterion-finite-over-target": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "statement": "Assume AC. Let $V=\\mathbb C^k$, $W=\\mathbb C^N$, $N\\ge1$, $k\\ge0$, and let $(B_1,B_2,I,J)$ satisfy $[B_1,B_2]+IJ=0$ and be stable, meaning that the vectors obtained by applying all words in $B_1,B_2$ to $I(W)$ span $V$. Costable means that no nonzero common $B_1,B_2$-invariant subspace lies in $\\ker J$, and regular means stable and costable. On $\\mathbb P^2$ with coordinates $[z_0:z_1:z_2]$, define\n\n$$\\alpha=\\begin{pmatrix}z_0B_1-z_1\\\\z_0B_2-z_2\\\\z_0J\\end{pmatrix}:V\\otimes\\mathcal O(-1)\\to(V\\oplus V\\oplus W)\\otimes\\mathcal O,\\qquad \\beta=\\begin{pmatrix}-z_0B_2+z_2&z_0B_1-z_1&z_0I\\end{pmatrix}:(V\\oplus V\\oplus W)\\otimes\\mathcal O\\to V\\otimes\\mathcal O(1).$$\n\nThen $\\beta\\alpha=z_0^2([B_1,B_2]+IJ)=0$, $\\alpha$ is injective as a sheaf map, and $\\beta$ is surjective at every point. The three-term complex is a monad, meaning it is exact at its first and last terms; its middle cohomology $\\mathcal E=\\ker\\beta/\\operatorname{im}\\alpha$ is torsion-free of rank $N$, has Hilbert polynomial $N\\binom{m+2}{2}-k$, and has a canonical framing $\\mathcal E|_{L_\\infty}\\cong W\\otimes\\mathcal O_{L_\\infty}$. Every framed torsion-free sheaf with this rank, framing and Hilbert polynomial arises uniquely up to simultaneous change of basis in $V$ from this construction. For a finite-type complex scheme $S$, a family of ADHM data is a rank-$k$ vector bundle with endomorphisms and framing maps satisfying the equation and stable on every geometric fiber; its monad cohomology is flat over $S$, and the two constructions are inverse on those families for which the relative cohomology bundles and base-change maps in the preceding Beilinson lemma have the stated ranks and are isomorphisms. A first-order framed deformation means an isomorphism class of such a family over $\\mathbb C[\\epsilon]/(\\epsilon^2)$ with its special fiber identified; the correspondence induces inverse maps on those deformation spaces and their tangent vector spaces. The sheaf is locally free exactly when the ADHM datum is costable; hence the regular locus is the locally-free sheaf locus. Combined with the real/complex quotient theorem, positive-FI data correspond to all such framed torsion-free sheaves, while the zero-FI real quotient corresponds to the locally-free locus. The framing representation has character $\\sum_{a=1}^N e_a^{-1}$ and the dual framing has character $\\sum_{a=1}^N e_a$.",
